# Terms that must never be committed to this repository.
#
# Copy this file to `.security-terms` and fill it in:
#
#     cp .security-terms.example .security-terms
#
# `.security-terms` is gitignored and stays on your machine. That is deliberate:
# a scanner that hardcodes the client's name would put the name in the public
# repository it exists to keep it out of (SEC-1). This example file is committed,
# so it must contain no real terms - placeholders only.
#
# One term per line. Matching is case-insensitive and substring-based, and each
# line is escaped before use, so punctuation and spaces are safe to include.
# Lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# What belongs here (see README, Data handling):
#   - the real organization's name, and any abbreviation or trading name
#   - real program and project names
#   - real contract numbers not already caught by the generic pattern
#   - names of real employees who appear in source documents
#   - internal system and platform names that were genericised in transcription
#
# The pre-commit hook refuses to run without this file, because the generic
# patterns cannot catch a company name and a scan that quietly skips a target is
# worse than one that stops.

# --- replace everything below with the real terms ---
ACME AEROSPACE
Acme
PROJECT PLACEHOLDER
